    slug: 'simplify-object-creation-with-builder-and-factory-pattern',
    title: 'Simplify object creation with the Builder and Factory patterns',
    description:
      'A practical introduction to the Builder and Factory patterns for creating objects in a clear and maintainable way.',
    date: '2026-03-21',
    draft: false,
    locale: 'en',
    body: 'The Builder and Factory patterns are two creational design patterns. Both simplify object creation, but they solve different problems.\n\n# 1. Builder pattern: structured creation\n\nThe Builder pattern makes the creation of a complex object more structured and fluent.\n\nInstead of initializing many properties in a constructor or object initializer, a builder lets us configure the object step by step.\n\nLet\'s say we have a `Person` class:\n\n```csharp\npublic class Person\n{\n    public string FirstName { get; set; }\n    public string LastName { get; set; }\n    public int Age { get; set; }\n    public double Size { get; set; }\n}\n```\n\nCreating an instance can look like this:\n\n```csharp\nvar person = new Person\n{\n    FirstName = "John",\n    LastName = "Doe",\n    Age = 24,\n    Size = 180.3\n};\n```\n\nThere is nothing wrong with this approach. However, as `Person` becomes more complex and gains more properties, the initialization can become difficult to read and maintain.\n\nThe same object can be created with a Builder:\n\n```csharp\npublic class PersonBuilder\n{\n    private readonly Person person = new();\n\n    public PersonBuilder WithFirstName(string name)\n    {\n        person.FirstName = name;\n        return this;\n    }\n\n    public PersonBuilder WithLastName(string name)\n    {\n        person.LastName = name;\n        return this;\n    }\n\n    public PersonBuilder WithAge(int age)\n    {\n        person.Age = age;\n        return this;\n    }\n\n    public PersonBuilder WithSize(double size)\n    {\n        person.Size = size;\n        return this;\n    }\n\n    public Person Build() => person;\n}\n```\n\nThe builder is responsible for assembling the object:\n\n```csharp\nvar person = new PersonBuilder()\n    .WithFirstName("John")\n    .WithLastName("Doe")\n    .WithAge(25)\n    .WithSize(185.6)\n    .Build();\n```\n\nThis syntax is readable and maintainable. The builder can also validate parameters before creating the final object.\n\n# 2. Factory pattern: hidden creation\n\nThe Factory pattern abstracts the creation of a complex object by hiding the creation logic behind a dedicated method.\n\nSuppose we have an `IAnimal` interface:\n\n```csharp\npublic interface IAnimal\n{\n    void Speak();\n}\n```\n\nTwo classes implement the interface:\n\n```csharp\npublic class Dog : IAnimal\n{\n    public void Speak()\n    {\n        Console.WriteLine("Woof woof");\n    }\n}\n\npublic class Cat : IAnimal\n{\n    public void Speak()\n    {\n        Console.WriteLine("Meow meow");\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThe factory hides which concrete class must be instantiated:\n\n```csharp\npublic static class AnimalFactory\n{\n    public static IAnimal Create(string type)\n    {\n        return type.ToLower() switch\n        {\n            "dog" => new Dog(),\n            "cat" => new Cat(),\n            _ => throw new ArgumentException("Invalid animal type")\n        };\n    }\n}\n```\n\nCreating an animal is now simple:\n\n```csharp\nIAnimal dog = AnimalFactory.Create("dog");\ndog.Speak(); // Woof woof\n```\n\nThe Factory pattern hides the complexity of object creation. It is particularly useful when the correct implementation depends on a configuration value or another runtime decision.\n\n# Summary\n\nThe Builder and Factory patterns both simplify object creation. The Builder structures the creation of a complex object, while the Factory hides which concrete object is created.\n\nThe Factory pattern focuses on **"What should I create?"** The Builder focuses on **"How do I create this complex object?"**',
    html: '<p>The Builder and Factory patterns are two creational design patterns. Both simplify object creation, but they solve different problems.</p><h1>1. Builder pattern: structured creation</h1><p>The Builder pattern makes the creation of a complex object more structured and fluent.</p><p>Instead of initializing many properties in a constructor or object initializer, a builder lets us configure the object step by step.</p><p>Let\'s say we have a <code>Person</code> class:</p><pre class="code-block"><code class="hljs language-csharp">public class Person\n{\n    public string FirstName { get; set; }\n    public string LastName { get; set; }\n    public int Age { get; set; }\n    public double Size { get; set; }\n}</code></pre><p>Creating an instance can look like this:</p><pre class="code-block"><code class="hljs language-csharp">var person = new Person\n{\n    FirstName = &quot;John&quot;,\n    LastName = &quot;Doe&quot;,\n    Age = 24,\n    Size = 180.3\n};</code></pre><p>There is nothing wrong with this approach. However, as <code>Person</code> becomes more complex and gains more properties, the initialization can become difficult to read and maintain.</p><p>The same object can be created with a Builder:</p><pre class="code-block"><code class="hljs language-csharp">public class PersonBuilder\n{\n    private readonly Person person = new();\n\n    public PersonBuilder WithFirstName(string name)\n    {\n        person.FirstName = name;\n        return this;\n    }\n\n    public PersonBuilder WithLastName(string name)\n    {\n        person.LastName = name;\n        return this;\n    }\n\n    public PersonBuilder WithAge(int age)\n    {\n        person.Age = age;\n        return this;\n    }\n\n    public PersonBuilder WithSize(double size)\n    {\n        person.Size = size;\n        return this;\n    }\n\n    public Person Build() =&gt; person;\n}</code></pre><p>The builder is responsible for assembling the object:</p><pre class="code-block"><code class="hljs language-csharp">var person = new PersonBuilder()\n    .WithFirstName(&quot;John&quot;)\n    .WithLastName(&quot;Doe&quot;)\n    .WithAge(25)\n    .WithSize(185.6)\n    .Build();</code></pre><p>This syntax is readable and maintainable. The builder can also validate parameters before creating the final object.</p><h1>2. Factory pattern: hidden creation</h1><p>The Factory pattern abstracts the creation of a complex object by hiding the creation logic behind a dedicated method.</p><p>Suppose we have an <code>IAnimal</code> interface:</p><pre class="code-block"><code class="hljs language-csharp">public interface IAnimal\n{\n    void Speak();\n}</code></pre><p>Two classes implement the interface:</p><pre class="code-block"><code class="hljs language-csharp">public class Dog : IAnimal\n{\n    public void Speak()\n    {\n        Console.WriteLine(&quot;Woof woof&quot;);\n    }\n}\n\npublic class Cat : IAnimal\n{\n    public void Speak()\n    {\n        Console.WriteLine(&quot;Meow meow&quot;);\n    }\n}</code></pre><p>The factory hides which concrete class must be instantiated:</p><pre class="code-block"><code class="hljs language-csharp">public static class AnimalFactory\n{\n    public static IAnimal Create(string type)\n    {\n        return type.ToLower() switch\n        {\n            &quot;dog&quot; =&gt; new Dog(),\n            &quot;cat&quot; =&gt; new Cat(),\n            _ =&gt; throw new ArgumentException(&quot;Invalid animal type&quot;)\n        };\n    }\n}</code></pre><p>Creating an animal is now simple:</p><pre class="code-block"><code class="hljs language-csharp">IAnimal dog = AnimalFactory.Create(&quot;dog&quot;);\ndog.Speak(); // Woof woof</code></pre><p>The Factory pattern hides the complexity of object creation. It is particularly useful when the correct implementation depends on a configuration value or another runtime decision.</p><h1>Summary</h1><p>The Builder and Factory patterns both simplify object creation. The Builder structures the creation of a complex object, while the Factory hides which concrete object is created.</p><p>The Factory pattern focuses on <strong>&quot;What should I create?&quot;</strong> The Builder focuses on <strong>&quot;How do I create this complex object?&quot;</strong></p>',
